# Expectation record for 07_variadics/004_va_copy_multiple_passes.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_va_copy_multiple_passes
area               = 07_variadics
description        = An argument list copied and traversed more than once, including a copy of a copy
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
The va_copy lifecycle is obeyed throughout: every copy has its own va_end, each list from
va_start is ended exactly once, a copy is always taken before its source has been
traversed so both start from the same position, a copy of a copy is legal, a list may be
ended without consuming all of its arguments, no list is read past the number of arguments
supplied, and every va_end sits in the same function as its va_start or va_copy.  Every
floating value is exactly representable, so the printed text is exact.  <stdarg.h> is
included because a variadic function cannot be written without it and it is in both
compilers' freestanding sets; printf is still hand-declared, because bcc ships no stdio.h.
END
